 ## Overview
-NeRV is a differentiable inverse rendering method that reconstructs a 3D scene representation from images under unconstrained, known lighting, enabling view synthesis and relighting with indirect illumination [S1]. It extends the Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) framework by decomposing the scene into volumetric density and material properties, and introduces a **Neural Visibility Field** to approximate light transport efficiently [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on brute-force raymarching or are limited to single point lights, NeRV uses learned neural approximations for visibility and surface intersections to reduce the complexity of direct and indirect lighting calculations from quadratic/cubic to linear/double-spherical integrals [S1].
+NeRV is a differentiable inverse rendering method that reconstructs a 3D scene representation from images under unconstrained, known lighting, enabling view synthesis and relighting with indirect illumination [S2]. It extends the Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) framework by decomposing the scene into volumetric density and material properties, and introduces a **Neural Visibility Field** to approximate light transport efficiently [S2]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on brute-force raymarching or are limited to single point lights, NeRV uses learned neural approximations for visibility and surface intersections to reduce the complexity of direct and indirect lighting calculations from quadratic/cubic to linear/double-spherical integrals [S2].
 
